Subject: AlienHand Multi-Mouse Input

Description: This specification defines the dual-pointer input substrate for AlienHand. Mouse A remains the ordinary Windows pointer. Mouse B is intended to be captured before Windows merges device input into the global cursor, then routed through per-target policy as blocked, integrated, or independent behavior.

@ [source] platform/source/SOP_Markdown.md
@ [reasoning] platform/refinement/Dual_Hemisphere_Thinking.sop
@ [programming] platform/source/ProgrammingGuide.md

& [AlienHandMultiMouseInput] is the input platform where one physical mouse can remain the Windows-owned pointer while another physical mouse becomes an AlienHand-owned virtual hand
  + [mouse_a] is the normal Windows pointer device
  + [mouse_b] is the AlienHand captured device
  + [capture_layer] is the lower-level backend that prevents [mouse_b] from moving the Windows cursor
  + [virtual_pointer_b] is the AlienHand state for [mouse_b]
  + [target_policy] is [TargetPointerMode] selected for the current target app, window, or surface
  + [legacy_output] is anonymous injected Windows mouse behavior for non-AlienHand surfaces
  + [independent_output] is source-aware AlienHand pointer behavior for AlienHand-aware surfaces

  = must: preserve [mouse_a] as the ordinary system pointer unless the user explicitly configures otherwise
  = must: identify [mouse_b] through a stable device identity before capture rules are applied
  = must: keep [mouse_b] from reaching the Windows pointer stream when it is captured
  = must: route captured [mouse_b] events through [TargetPointerMode]
  = must: distinguish true AlienHand independent pointer behavior from legacy Windows event injection
  = should: support per-target and later per-surface policy overrides
  - never: claim that legacy Windows apps receive a true second OS pointer

| [TargetPointerMode] is (blocked | integrated | independent)
  + [use_layer] is exclusive [TargetPointerMode]
  = must: keep the value vocabulary closed until a new mode has a defined runtime contract

& [TargetPointerPolicy] is the per-target decision record for captured pointer behavior
  + [mode] is exclusive [TargetPointerMode]
  + [legacy_fallback] is blocked or integrated when [mode] is independent but the target cannot receive source-aware AlienHand events
  + [target_identity] is app, process, window, and optional AlienHand surface identity
  + [policy_rule] is a reusable selector for target_id, process_name, window_title_contains, or surface_id
  + [specificity] is the rule-ranking policy that lets surface-specific rules override app-level rules

  = must: default unknown targets to a safe behavior
  = must: apply [legacy_fallback] when independent mode is configured for a legacy target
  = must: allow process-level app policy and surface-specific override policy
  = must: serialize and reload policy tables as JSON for prototype scope
  = should: support policy persistence after the prototype router is proven

& [TwoPointerHybridInput] is the working model for real operation
  + [windows_pointer] follows [mouse_a]
  + [alienhand_pointer] follows [mouse_b]
  + [relative_motion] is Raw Input mouse movement expressed as dx and dy
  + [absolute_virtual_position] is the clamped AlienHand pointer location derived from [relative_motion]
  + [alienhand_surface] receives [alienhand_pointer] as independent source-aware events
  + [legacy_surface] receives [alienhand_pointer] through [LegacyMouseMultiplexing] when policy is integrated

  = must: update [virtual_pointer_b] even when delivery is blocked
  = must: normalize relative motion into absolute virtual coordinates before routing
  = must: clamp virtual pointer coordinates to the active screen or surface bounds
  = must: render or expose [virtual_pointer_b] state independently from the Windows pointer
  = should: hide, restore, or mask cursor jumps when integrated legacy injection is used

& [LegacyMouseMultiplexing] is the compatibility strategy for non-AlienHand apps
  + [input] is captured [mouse_b] virtual pointer state
  + [output] is an anonymous Windows mouse event stream
  + [cursor_policy] is move, restore, hide, or overlay-mask behavior around injection
  + [gesture_owner] is the currently serialized captured pointer gesture
  + [recording_backend] is the current proof-only backend that records intended legacy injection actions without moving the real cursor

  = must: serialize legacy gestures that depend on global cursor capture or button state
  = must: inject only ordinary legacy mouse events into legacy targets
  = must: preserve [virtual_pointer_b] state before and after injection
  = must: plan move before button or wheel actions so the legacy target receives the action at [virtual_pointer_b] coordinates
  = must: keep proof backends non-invasive until explicit real-input permission and backend support exist
  = should: coalesce move events when high-frequency alternation would reduce stability
  - never: allow simultaneous independent drags to be promised in legacy mode

& [CaptureLayerBoundary] is the driver/interception seam not implemented by the current Python proof
  + [raw_input_scan] is the user-mode ability to observe distinct Raw Input devices
  + [device_assignment] is the explicit mapping from Raw Input device name to AlienHand logical mouse identity
  + [capture_backend] is a future driver, filter, or interception backend that can consume a chosen device before Windows cursor merge
  + [injection_backend] is the future implementation that converts integrated events into Windows-compatible input

  = must: treat Raw Input device discovery as identity evidence, not capture
  = must: keep device assignment as suggested_only until a user reviews or a later trusted policy activates it
  = must: keep capture/block claims provisional until a backend proves consumed input no longer reaches the system pointer
  = should: keep the router independent from the eventual capture backend

& [VirtualizedInputTestLayer] is the hardware-free proof layer for target policy and routing behavior
  + [implementation] is AlienHand/python/alienhand_ai/multi_mouse.py
  + [tests] are AlienHand/python/tests/test_multi_mouse.py
  + [proof_command] is multi-mouse-routing-proof
  + [device_scan_command] is multi-mouse-device-scan
  + [assignment_suggestion_command] is multi-mouse-assignment-suggestion
  + [policy_persistence] is read_policy_table and write_policy_table
  + [assignment_persistence] is read_device_assignment_table and write_device_assignment_table
  + [motion_normalization] is VirtualPointerTracker, ScreenBounds, and PointerDeltaPacket
  + [legacy_injection_plan] is RecordingLegacyInjectionBackend and legacy_injection_actions

  = must: simulate [mouse_a] as the Windows pointer
  = must: simulate [mouse_b] as a captured AlienHand pointer
  = must: prove blocked, integrated, independent, and independent-to-legacy fallback routing
  = must: prove app-level and surface-specific policy resolution without hardware
  = must: prove policy table JSON round trip without hardware
  = must: prove suggested physical-device assignment without activating capture
  = must: prove assignment table JSON round trip without hardware
  = must: prove relative delta normalization into clamped absolute virtual pointer events
  = must: prove legacy injection action planning without moving the real cursor
  = must: preserve inspectable routed event records
  = must: avoid moving the real cursor or blocking real hardware during simulation
  = should: provide a live Raw Input scan to check how many physical mouse devices Windows exposes

& [MultiMouseVirtualRoutingProof] is the current smallest truth test for the dual-pointer architecture
  + [one_windows_mouse] is mouse-a
  + [one_captured_mouse] is mouse-b
  + [one_blocked_target] is secure-login
  + [one_legacy_target] is legacy-editor
  + [one_alienhand_target] is alienhand-workbench
  + [one_fallback_target] is legacy-independent-request

  = must: route [mouse_a] to windows_passthrough
  = must: route blocked [mouse_b] events to blocked with delivered false
  = must: route independent [mouse_b] events to alienhand_independent with pointer identity
  = must: route integrated [mouse_b] events to legacy_injected
  = must: route independent [mouse_b] events for a legacy target through legacy_fallback
  = must: leave the Windows pointer state controlled only by [mouse_a]
  = verify: routing_modes_ok is true

& [MultiMouseDeviceScanProof] is the non-invasive hardware visibility check
  + [implementation] is AlienHand/python/alienhand_ai/multi_mouse.py enumerate_windows_raw_input_devices
  + [proof_command] is multi-mouse-device-scan
  + [evidence] is Raw Input device list grouped by mouse, keyboard, hid, or unknown

  = must: list Raw Input devices without capture or blocking
  = must: report [multiple_mice_visible] when two or more mouse-class devices are visible
  = must: report [capture_backend_present] false until a real capture backend exists
  - never: use this scan as proof that AlienHand can consume one mouse before Windows cursor merge

& [MultiMouseAssignmentSuggestionProof] is the safe mapping step from physical mouse devices to logical AlienHand roles
  + [implementation] is AlienHand/python/alienhand_ai/multi_mouse.py suggest_mouse_device_assignments
  + [proof_command] is multi-mouse-assignment-suggestion
  + [roles] are windows_pointer, alienhand_pointer, and observed
  + [activation_state] is suggested_only

  = must: assign the first visible mouse as mouse-a windows_pointer candidate
  = must: assign the second visible mouse as mouse-b alienhand_pointer candidate
  = must: assign extra visible mice as observed until reviewed
  = must: expose captured_device_ids without activating capture
  = must: serialize the assignment table for review
  - never: silently activate capture from a suggested assignment
